QUESTIONS.push(
/* ---------------- Why pharmacogenomics: history ---------------- */
{id:"L17-001", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"history", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"interindividual-variation-factors", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Large inter-individual variations in drug response were already being linked to several factors before genetics could explain them. Which factors are listed? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Age", correct:true, why:"Listed on the slide and named first in her sentence."},
  {t:"Ethnicity", correct:true, why:"Listed and spoken."},
  {t:"Gender", correct:true, why:"Listed and spoken."},
  {t:"Underlying disease", correct:true, why:"Listed and spoken."},
  {t:"Genetic variation", correct:true, why:"The last bullet, and the one pharmacogenomics adds: with more knowledge of genetics, some variation is now explained by genes."},
  {t:"Time of day the dose is taken", correct:false, why:"Not one of the factors on the list."},
  {t:"Brand versus generic product", correct:false, why:"Not one of the factors on the list."}],
 teach:"Pharmacogenomics exists because people respond very differently to the same drug, sometimes a lethal side effect in one person and no effect in another. Those inter-individual variations were first associated with age, ethnicity, gender and underlying disease. As knowledge of genes grew, some of the variation could be explained by genetic variation, the fifth factor on the list.",
 quote:"we could see that they were associated with age and ethnicity and gender and underlying disease. So we were seeing that already, sometimes explaining. Inter-individual variability and drug response based on those factors, but like I've just said, with our progression and our knowledge about genetics, we are now able to explain some of these variations in response, uh, based on genetics.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Introduction: Inter-Individual Variations’ (p. 4); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-002", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"history", skill:"recall",
 concept:"primaquine-first-indication", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What was one of the first indications that people respond to a medication differently based on their genetics?",
 options:[
  {t:"Primaquine, an antimalarial, in the Korean War", correct:true, why:"Primaquine was used as an antimalarial prophylactic in the 1950s during the Korean War, and some soldiers responded very badly to it."},
  {t:"Digoxin toxicity in heart failure patients in the 1950s", correct:false, why:"Digoxin came up as the P-glycoprotein example, not as the first historical data point."},
  {t:"Antipyrine half-lives in twins", correct:false, why:"The twin studies came later, around the time of the Human Genome Project."},
  {t:"Warfarin bleeding in the 1990s", correct:false, why:"Warfarin is a later example she has deferred."},
  {t:"Methanol blindness in World War II", correct:false, why:"Methanol was a toxicology example, not a pharmacogenomics history point."}],
 teach:"Primaquine is an antimalarial used as a prophylactic, and it was given widely to military personnel in the field during the Korean War in the 1950s. Clinicians saw that some soldiers developed hemolytic anemia on it and that the bad responses fell along the lines of ethnicity. That was the first real data point linking a bad drug response to an inherited trait.",
 quote:"So one of the first indications that we had that people were responding to medications differently compared to each other based on genetics was when we're using Primaquin. Um, Primaquin is an anti-malarial medication. Um, we use it as an anti-malarial prophylactic medication and so, uh, we were using this a lot in the 50s during the Korean War.",
 note:"The transcript renders primaquine as \"Primaquin\", \"Prima Quinn\" and \"Primaquine\".",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Introduction: History of Primaquine’ (p. 5); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-003", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"history", skill:"apply",
 concept:"primaquine-g6pd", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Soldiers given primaquine who developed hemolytic anemia were found to share which characteristic?",
 options:[
  {t:"Low activity of glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD)", correct:true, why:"Those who responded badly had low activity of this enzyme in their red blood cells; most were of Middle Eastern or African descent."},
  {t:"Low activity of cytochrome P450 2C19 in the liver", correct:false, why:"CYP2C19 is her running example for genotype; it was not the primaquine finding."},
  {t:"A low-activity version of the P-glycoprotein efflux pump", correct:false, why:"P-glycoprotein was the digoxin example."},
  {t:"High activity of alcohol dehydrogenase in the liver", correct:false, why:"Alcohol dehydrogenase belongs to the methanol and ethylene glycol story."},
  {t:"A variant HLA antigen on their white blood cells", correct:false, why:"HLA variants are the newest target area, not the primaquine finding."}],
 teach:"In the Korean War, clinicians noticed that people of Middle Eastern or African descent were the ones developing hemolytic anemia on primaquine. When the activity of glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase was measured in their red blood cells, those who had responded badly had low activity of the enzyme. The slide adds that the hemolytic anemia followed significant methemoglobinemia.",
 quote:"The clinicians there in that Korean War setting were astute enough to observe that it was people of Middle Eastern or of African descent seemed to be the ones that were having this bad response to Primaquin. ... people that had a deficiency in the activity of the enzyme glucose 6 phosphate dehydrogenase, those were the ones that were responding badly to primaquin",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Introduction: History of Primaquine’ (p. 5); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-004", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"history", skill:"apply",
 concept:"primaquine-phenotype-not-genotype", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"In the 1950s primaquine finding, what was actually measured, and why does it still count as evidence about genes?",
 options:[
  {t:"Enzyme activity, a phenotype that reflected their genotype", correct:true, why:"Genotyping was not possible then; enzyme activity could be measured, and that phenotype was known to reflect a genetic variation."},
  {t:"Their DNA sequence, read directly from blood samples", correct:false, why:"No one could genotype at that time."},
  {t:"Drug blood levels, which are set entirely by genes", correct:false, why:"Blood levels were not the measurement, and they are not set entirely by genes."},
  {t:"Family histories, which identified the variant allele directly", correct:false, why:"The measurement was enzyme activity in red blood cells."},
  {t:"Nothing; the link to genes was only a guess", correct:false, why:"The enzyme activity was the measurement that linked the response to a genetic variation."}],
 teach:"At the time no one could genotype a patient, but the activity of an enzyme could be measured. Low glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase activity in red blood cells was a phenotype, and enough genetics was known to say that the phenotype reflected a genetic variation. Primaquine is therefore the first data point linking a bad drug response to a phenotype that stands for a genotype.",
 quote:"Like I said before, we couldn't genotype at that time, but we could measure that enzyme activity, and we realized that that phenotype of the enzyme activity was reflecting these people's genotype, OK?",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Introduction: History of Primaquine’ (p. 5); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-005", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"history", skill:"figure", type:"match",
 concept:"pgx-timeline", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Match each year on the genomics history timeline with what happened.",
 left:["1953", "1956", "1957", "1959", "1990", "2003"],
 right:["Watson and Crick describe the structure of DNA", "A genetic link to hemolytic reactions to primaquine", "Motulsky: inheritance might explain individual differences in drug efficacy and adverse reactions", "Vogel introduces the term pharmacogenetics", "The Human Genome Project starts", "The Human Genome Project is completed"],
 pairs:[
  {l:"1953", r:"Watson and Crick describe the structure of DNA", why:"The start of her timeline; the slide says DNA's double helix."},
  {l:"1956", r:"A genetic link to hemolytic reactions to primaquine", why:"The primaquine story, in its year."},
  {l:"1957", r:"Motulsky: inheritance might explain individual differences in drug efficacy and adverse reactions", why:"\"just a year later\"; eye opening to medicine at the time."},
  {l:"1959", r:"Vogel introduces the term pharmacogenetics", why:"The first word linking drugs to genetics."},
  {l:"1990", r:"The Human Genome Project starts", why:"A worldwide consortium of academics, industry and government agencies."},
  {l:"2003", r:"The Human Genome Project is completed", why:"Early and under budget."}],
 teach:"The timeline runs from the structure of DNA (1953) to the genetic link to primaquine reactions (1956), Motulsky's statement that inheritance might explain differences in drug efficacy and adverse reactions (1957) and Vogel's term pharmacogenetics (1959). The Human Genome Project started in 1990 and was completed in 2003, early and under budget; the slide marks renewed interest in pharmacogenetics after that.",
 quote:"starting with 1953 when Watson and Crick and other scientists, they described the structure of DNA. ... So it wasn't until 1956 that they found that genetic link to the hemolytic anemia in patients responding badly to Primaquine. ... All right, then in 1990, the Human Genome Project started. ... OK, so in 2003, the Human Genome Project was completed and it was completed early and under budget, which is amazing.",
 note:"The transcript renders Motulsky as \"Motosky\" and \"Muussky\"; the slide spells \"Motulsky\". The timeline figure is from DiPiro's Pharmacotherapy, 7th edition.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Introduction: Genomics History’ (p. 6, DiPiro 7th ed. figure); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-006", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"history", skill:"recall",
 concept:"vogel-coined-pharmacogenetics", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Who introduced the term pharmacogenetics, and in what year?",
 options:[
  {t:"Friedrich Vogel, 1959", correct:true, why:"The first word coined to link pharmacology, or drugs, to genetics."},
  {t:"Motulsky, 1957", correct:false, why:"Motulsky made the statement about inheritance explaining differences in drug efficacy and adverse reactions; he did not coin the term."},
  {t:"Watson and Crick, 1953", correct:false, why:"They described the structure of DNA."},
  {t:"The Human Genome Project, 2003", correct:false, why:"2003 is the year the project was completed."},
  {t:"The AAPS focus group, 1990", correct:false, why:"The AAPS group supplied the modern definitions on the definitions slide; 1990 is the start of the Human Genome Project."}],
 teach:"In 1959 Friedrich Vogel introduced the term pharmacogenetics, the first word linking drugs to genetics: how a person's genotype affects their response to medications. Two years earlier Motulsky had proposed that inheritance might explain many individual differences in drug efficacy and adverse drug reactions, which was eye opening to medicine then.",
 quote:"All right, then in 1959, Frederick Vogel, he introduced the term pharmacogenetics. So that was the first word that he coined to link. Pharmacology or drugs to genetics, OK?",
 note:"Spellings differ: slide \"Fredrich Vogel\"; transcript \"Frederick Vogel\" and \"Friedrich Vogel\". The slide spells \"Motulsky\".",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Introduction: Genomics History’ (p. 6); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-007", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"history", skill:"recall",
 concept:"hgp-data-sharing", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"What made the Human Genome Project, in her words, one of the most amazing scientific projects?",
 options:[
  {t:"Labs worldwide shared each sequence in an open database", correct:true, why:"Once a lab sequenced its stretch of DNA, it went into a database everybody could access; she called that one of the most amazing parts."},
  {t:"A single government lab sequenced the whole genome in secret", correct:false, why:"It was a worldwide consortium of academics, industry and government agencies."},
  {t:"It produced whole genome sequencing in hours by 1995", correct:false, why:"Whole genome sequencing came much more recently, after the project."},
  {t:"It finished late but sequenced every human alive", correct:false, why:"It finished early and under budget in 2003."},
  {t:"It was run only by drug companies to find targets", correct:false, why:"Academics, industry and government agencies all took part."}],
 teach:"The Human Genome Project (1990 to 2003) was a worldwide consortium of academics, industry and government agencies, each lab sequencing a stretch of DNA. Unlike usual research, every sequence went into a shared database. It finished early and under budget, and the faster methods developed during it set the stage for whole genome sequencing, now possible in a matter of hours, which came later.",
 quote:"It was a worldwide consortium. It involved academics. It involved industry, it involved government agencies. ... So once one lab discovered the DNA sequence for one chunk of chunk of the human genome, it was put up in a database and everybody had access to it. So that I think is one of the most amazing parts of this project.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Introduction: Genomics History’ (p. 6); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-008", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"history", skill:"figure",
 concept:"twin-study-heritability", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In the twin study figure of antipyrine half-life, what pattern shows that drug metabolism is heritable?",
 options:[
  {t:"Identical pairs match; fraternal pairs vary", correct:true, why:"Almost every identical twin pair had the same half-life; the fraternal pairs were variable, with only pair d close."},
  {t:"Fraternal pairs match; identical pairs vary", correct:false, why:"The reverse of the figure."},
  {t:"Both kinds of pair vary to the same degree", correct:false, why:"Then genes would not explain the variation."},
  {t:"Identical twins clear the drug faster", correct:false, why:"The comparison is within pairs, not the speed of one group against the other."},
  {t:"Every pair has the same half-life", correct:false, why:"The half-lives differ widely between pairs and within fraternal pairs."}],
 teach:"Twin studies compared identical twins with fraternal twins given the same drug. Antipyrine half-lives were nearly identical within identical twin pairs but varied within fraternal pairs, which behave almost like unrelated people. The figure (Goodman and Gilman Fig. 4-2) shows the heritability of drug metabolism variability.",
 quote:"and you can see almost all of our identical twin pairs, their clearance is exactly the same. There's just a few that are a little bit different. ... That's different if you look at the fraternal twin pairs, you see, they are often, all of them are very variable. Only this, this pair here, pair D, is a little bit similar",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Introduction: Twin Studies Data’ (p. 7, G&G Fig. 4-2); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-009", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"history", skill:"apply",
 concept:"twin-study-antipyrine-phenotype", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Why was antipyrine a good drug for the classic twin study, and what did the study measure?",
 options:[
  {t:"Cleared by one enzyme; half-life, a phenotype", correct:true, why:"Antipyrine is cleared entirely by metabolism by one enzyme, so the phenotype was clear; the twins were not genotyped."},
  {t:"Cleared by the kidney; creatinine, a phenotype", correct:false, why:"Antipyrine is cleared by metabolism."},
  {t:"Cleared by one enzyme; each twin's genotype", correct:false, why:"No one was genotyped; the half-life was the measurement."},
  {t:"Cleared by many enzymes; each twin's response", correct:false, why:"One enzyme made the data clean."},
  {t:"Not metabolized at all; blood pressure", correct:false, why:"Antipyrine is cleared all the way by metabolism."}],
 teach:"Antipyrine is cleared entirely by metabolism, essentially by one enzyme, so a twin's half-life gave a clean phenotype for whether that enzyme was working in the normal range. The twins were not genotyped; the studies looked only at the phenotype, the half-life of clearance. Genotyping came later and made such studies more sophisticated.",
 quote:"That I like to point out what they were doing is they were looking at antipyrene Half-Life. This is a medication that um is cleared all the way by metabolism. It's just like one enzyme does it. ... We did not genotype these people at this time. We were looking at their phenotype, which was the half-life of the clearance of the drug, OK?",
 note:"The transcript renders antipyrine as \"antipyrene\".",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Introduction: Twin Studies Data’ (p. 7); transcript 10/20"},

/* ---------------- Pharmacogenetics vs pharmacogenomics ---------------- */
{id:"L17-010", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"defs", skill:"term",
 concept:"pgenetics-vs-pgenomics", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"How does pharmacogenetics differ from pharmacogenomics?",
 options:[
  {t:"One patient vs populations, genome-wide", correct:true, why:"Pharmacogenetics is the term for an individual; pharmacogenomics is the broader, genome-wide term for populations."},
  {t:"Drug metabolism only vs drug targets only", correct:false, why:"The difference is scope (individual vs population), not which genes."},
  {t:"Inherited variants vs variants acquired during life", correct:false, why:"Not the distinction on the slide."},
  {t:"Animal studies vs human studies", correct:false, why:"Not the distinction on the slide."},
  {t:"The older term vs the term for gene therapy only", correct:false, why:"Pharmacogenomics also covers technologies such as gene therapy, but that is not its only meaning."}],
 teach:"Pharmacogenetics is the study of genetic causes of individual variations in drug response: one patient and their response to a drug. Pharmacogenomics more broadly involves genome-wide analysis of the genetic determinants of drug efficacy and toxicity, in populations, and also covers genomics technologies such as bioengineered proteins and gene therapy. The two are used interchangeably; the preferred term is pharmacogenomics.",
 quote:"OK, so, pharmacogenetics is the term that we use when we're thinking about an individual, one patient, and their response to a drug, OK. Pharmacogenomics is a more global term. That's where we're thinking about populations as a whole or studying these concepts within populations or comparing populations.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Introduction: Definition of Genomics vs Genetics’ (p. 8); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-011", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"defs", skill:"recall",
 concept:"pgx-preferred-term-aaps", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which statement about the two terms is correct?",
 options:[
  {t:"Used interchangeably; pharmacogenomics is preferred", correct:true, why:"The slide's last bullet; the definitions are from the AAPS (American Association of Pharmaceutical Scientists) Pharmacogenomics Focus Group."},
  {t:"Never interchangeable; pharmacogenetics is preferred", correct:false, why:"They are used interchangeably, even by people renowned in the field."},
  {t:"Gene therapy belongs under pharmacogenetics", correct:false, why:"Genomics technologies such as gene therapy fall under pharmacogenomics."},
  {t:"Both terms were defined only by the FDA", correct:false, why:"The slide's definitions are the AAPS focus group's; the WHO also defines them."},
  {t:"Pharmacogenomics is the older of the two words", correct:false, why:"Pharmacogenetics was the term Vogel introduced in 1959."}],
 teach:"The two terms technically differ, but they are often used interchangeably, and she said she does not always switch between them properly; most of the time she says pharmacogenomics. The slide's definitions come from the American Association of Pharmaceutical Scientists (AAPS) Pharmacogenomics Focus Group; the World Health Organization also publishes definitions. Gene therapy is an example of a pharmacogenomic technology.",
 quote:"So they're often used interchangeably by people that are, you know, renowned in the field, but that's OK. Oh, I'm sorry, this one is the American Association of Pharmaceutical Scientists, uh, uh, pharmacogenomics focus group that defined these terms",
 note:"She first said \"if I recall, these are World Health Organization definitions\" and then corrected herself to the AAPS focus group, which is the slide's reference. Keyed on AAPS.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Introduction: Definition of Genomics vs Genetics’ (p. 8); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-012", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"defs", skill:"apply",
 concept:"why-organizations-define-terms", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Why would a big organization such as the AAPS or the WHO (World Health Organization) publish official definitions of scientific terms?",
 options:[
  {t:"To get everyone on the same page", correct:true, why:"Her answer: shared definitions give better scientific communication, so data from one country's agency can be compared with another's."},
  {t:"To control who may sell genetic tests", correct:false, why:"Not the reason she gave."},
  {t:"To replace the textbooks' definitions with newer ones", correct:false, why:"Not the reason she gave."},
  {t:"To decide which drugs must be genotyped before use", correct:false, why:"Not the reason she gave."},
  {t:"To keep pharmacists and prescribers using different words", correct:false, why:"The point is common language."}],
 teach:"Agencies across the world publish definitions so that everyone means the same thing. If drug companies, regulators and researchers define genotypes or sample pharmacogenomic populations differently, the data get messy and one country's agency cannot compare its data with another's. Common definitions give better scientific communication.",
 quote:"Why would the, why would a big organization like the AAPS or the WHO, why would they define terms like this? What do you think the world would define terms, scientific terms. It helps get us all on the same page, OK ... so that's the reason why the world gets together and does some of these definitions is that we have better scientific communication going on.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Introduction: Definition of Genomics vs Genetics’ (p. 8); transcript 10/20"},

/* ---------------- Impact, uses and limitations ---------------- */
{id:"L17-013", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"impact", skill:"recall",
 concept:"mackinnon-more-than-half", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What did the MacKinnon et al. (2023) study find?",
 options:[
  {t:"Over half were on drugs where pharmacogenomics matters", correct:true, why:"Thousands of patients' records were reviewed; more than half were prescribed medications where pharmacogenomics is important in their use."},
  {t:"Pharmacists feel unconfident about using pharmacogenomics", correct:false, why:"That is the Wondrasek et al. (2024) finding."},
  {t:"Pharmacogenomics was used in most of those prescriptions", correct:false, why:"The study did not evaluate whether pharmacogenomics was used."},
  {t:"Fewer than 1% of patients had a relevant variant", correct:false, why:"Not the finding; 1% is the polymorphism cutoff."},
  {t:"Genotyping before every prescription saved money", correct:false, why:"Cost was not part of the study."}],
 teach:"MacKinnon et al. (2023) pulled the medical records of thousands of patients and found that more than half were prescribed medications where pharmacogenomics is important in their use. The study did not evaluate whether pharmacogenomics was actually used when those patients were prescribed and treated.",
 quote:"Uh, McKinnon found, wrote, did a study, and they found that more than half of the patients evaluated were prescribed medications where pharmacogenomics is important in their use. ... They did not evaluate whether pharmacogenomics was used as these patients were prescribed and treated",
 note:"The transcript renders MacKinnon as \"McKinnon\".",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Introduction: Impact of Pharmacogenomics’ (p. 9); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-014", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"impact", skill:"recall",
 concept:"wondrasek-pharmacists-confidence", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What did Wondrasek et al. (2024) find when they asked pharmacists about pharmacogenomics?",
 options:[
  {t:"Important, but they are not confident using it", correct:true, why:"The slide's second bullet, read aloud."},
  {t:"They think it is unimportant and do not use it", correct:false, why:"Pharmacists do feel it is important."},
  {t:"They feel confident and use it with most patients", correct:false, why:"They do not feel confident in their knowledge or ability to use it."},
  {t:"They prefer to leave it to prescribers", correct:false, why:"Prescribers are less versed in it than pharmacists."},
  {t:"They had never heard of it", correct:false, why:"Not the finding."}],
 teach:"Wondrasek et al. (2024), a year after MacKinnon, asked pharmacists about their knowledge and comfort: although pharmacists feel pharmacogenomics is important, they do not feel confident in their knowledge of it or their ability to use it. Her aim for the next several days is to bring the class to at least a basic comfort zone.",
 quote:"All right, then this one by Wandrasek a year later, they were talking with pharmacists about their knowledge, their comfort with pharmacogenomics. And although pharmacists feel pharmacogenomics is important, they don't feel confident in their knowledge or their ability to use it, OK?",
 note:"The transcript renders Wondrasek as \"Wandrasek\".",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Introduction: Impact of Pharmacogenomics’ (p. 9); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-015", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"impact", skill:"recall",
 concept:"prescribers-less-versed", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Which other health care professionals should know pharmacogenomics, and how well educated in it are they compared with pharmacists?",
 options:[
  {t:"Prescribers; they are less versed in it", correct:true, why:"Her answer to her own question: anybody prescribing; prescribers spend less time with drugs, and pharmacy students are the best educated in pharmacogenomics."},
  {t:"Prescribers; they are better educated in it", correct:false, why:"Pharmacy students and pharmacists are the best educated."},
  {t:"Only laboratory geneticists; nobody else needs it", correct:false, why:"Anybody prescribing, nurses and other providers should know it."},
  {t:"Nurses; they order most genetic tests", correct:false, why:"Nurses were named among those who should know it, not as the ones ordering tests."},
  {t:"No one; it is still only a research tool", correct:false, why:"She expects pharmacogenomics in every area of pharmacy practice."}],
 teach:"Anybody who prescribes should know pharmacogenomics, and so should nurses and other providers. Prescribers are not yet as well versed in it as pharmacists, because they spend less time with drugs. Pharmacy students are the best educated in pharmacogenomics, and pharmacists have a big role in caring for patients from this angle.",
 quote:"Can you think of other healthcare professions that M should know about pharmacogenomics. How about anybody that's prescribing? OK. ... But people that are prescribing so far, uh, our prescribers are not as well versed yet in pharmacogenomics, cause they don't spend as much time with drugs as you guys do.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Introduction: Impact of Pharmacogenomics’ (p. 9); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-016", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"impact", skill:"apply",
 concept:"genotype-before-lethal-drug", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A student asked whether a variant can be found before a reaction occurs. When is it better to genotype the patient before giving the drug?",
 options:[
  {t:"When the drug can quickly cause a lethal side effect", correct:true, why:"Then you genotype first; for some medications that is standard protocol, and you could be liable if you did not."},
  {t:"Never; genotyping is done only after a reaction occurs", correct:false, why:"Reacting after the fact is acceptable only when the side effect is not lethal."},
  {t:"Only when the drug fails to work after a month", correct:false, why:"Lack of efficacy can prompt genotyping afterward; that is not the case for testing first."},
  {t:"For every drug, whatever its toxicity", correct:false, why:"She separated drugs that should be genotyped from those that could be but usually are not."},
  {t:"Only when the patient asks for it", correct:false, why:"Severity of the possible toxicity drives the decision."}],
 teach:"Often a patient's response, a side effect or a lack of efficacy, prompts a look at their genotype afterward, which is acceptable when the side effect is not lethal. When a drug can quickly cause a lethal side effect, the patient should be genotyped first; for some medications that is standard protocol and a clinician could be liable for not doing it. Which drugs should be genotyped, and which could be but rarely are, is a clinical-use issue she will cover later; her view is that if insurance will pay and it saves cost, do it.",
 quote:"But what if you're giving a drug that somebody could quickly develop a lethal side effect? It's better to genotype the patient, figure out what version of a gene they have before you give them that drug ... It, it is, yes, it is for some medications, it's standard protocol. You could be liable. If you didn't genotype patients before we give them some drugs.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Polymorphism’ (p. 14, student question); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-017", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"impact", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"pgx-practice-areas", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Which practice areas were named as places where pharmacogenomics is big or growing? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Oncology", correct:true, why:"\"you'll be using pharmacogenomics all the time\"."},
  {t:"Central nervous system (CNS)", correct:true, why:"\"CNS pharmacogenomics is big\"."},
  {t:"Cardiology", correct:true, why:"\"Some cardio ... pharmacogenomics is big\"."},
  {t:"Infectious disease", correct:true, why:"\"doing more and more of pharmacogenomics\"."},
  {t:"None yet; it is limited to research", correct:false, why:"She expects it in every area of pharmacy to some degree."}],
 teach:"She expects pharmacogenomics to be part of practice in every area of pharmacy to some degree. Oncology uses it all the time; it is big in CNS and cardiology, and infectious disease uses more and more of it. The field moves fast, so the course gives the basics and a way of thinking to keep learning after graduation.",
 quote:"Some people, especially if you like choose oncology, you'll be using pharmacogenomics all the time. CNS pharmacogenomics is big. Some cardio pharma pharma, I mean pharmacogenomics is big. Infectious disease, doing more and more of pharmacogenomics, OK?",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Introduction: Genomics History’ (p. 6); transcript 10/20"},

/* ---------------- The four target areas ---------------- */
{id:"L17-018", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"targets", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"four-target-areas", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which are the target areas of pharmacogenomics that she said you need to know? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Metabolizing enzymes", correct:true, why:"The first area, where the most is known: P450s and other phase 1 and phase 2 enzymes."},
  {t:"Drug transporters", correct:true, why:"The second area; P-glycoprotein is the example."},
  {t:"Drug targets", correct:true, why:"The third area: receptors or enzymes a drug acts on."},
  {t:"Immune system targets", correct:true, why:"The fourth, newest area: HLA antigen variants."},
  {t:"Plasma protein binding", correct:false, why:"Not one of the four."},
  {t:"Renal filtration", correct:false, why:"Not one of the four."}],
 teach:"The four target areas are metabolizing enzymes, drug transporters, drug targets and immunological (immune system) targets. They frame every pharmacogenomics example that follows. The slide is titled with three areas; she changed the content that morning to four and said she forgot to change the title.",
 quote:"So these are the four areas that we'll cover as we go through examples of pharmacogenomics, OK? You do need to know these. So what are they? Metabolizing enzymes, transporters, drug targets, and then immunological targets, OK?",
 note:"Conflict: the slide title reads \"Three Target Areas\" and lists three bullets; she said \"change this from 3 to 4 target areas\" and added immune system targets. Keyed on four.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Introduction: Three Target Areas’ (p. 10); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-019", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"targets", skill:"apply",
 concept:"metabolizing-enzymes-low-hanging-fruit", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Why do we know the most about genetic variants in metabolizing enzymes?",
 options:[
  {t:"They were the low hanging fruit", correct:true, why:"Patients who could not clear certain drugs had blood concentrations too high, sometimes lethal; those obvious, dangerous reactions were studied first."},
  {t:"They are the only genes that have variants", correct:false, why:"Transporters, drug targets and immune genes have variants too."},
  {t:"Transporter genes could not be sequenced", correct:false, why:"Transporter genes have been identified and many variants defined."},
  {t:"Their variants affect only efficacy, not toxicity", correct:false, why:"Their variants caused the serious toxic reactions."},
  {t:"Their variants are always rare variants", correct:false, why:"Frequency was not the reason given."}],
 teach:"Variants in metabolizing enzymes, P450s first and now other phase 1 and phase 2 enzymes, cause differences in drug plasma concentrations between people. Some patients could not clear certain drugs, their blood concentrations went too high and some died; once genotyping was possible, those were the most apparent and most dangerous reactions to explain. The slide adds that this may have serious implications for narrow therapeutic index drugs.",
 quote:"Uh, the reason why we know the most about variants in metabolizing enzymes is because they were really the low hanging fruit. ... we had some medications that if people couldn't metabolize and clear those drugs, they were ending up with their blood drug, drug blood concentrations being too high, and you sometimes were killing patients because they weren't clearing the drugs, OK.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Introduction: Three Target Areas’ (p. 10); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-020", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"targets", skill:"recall",
 concept:"pgp-abcb1-efflux", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Can you think of a drug transporter expressed in the gut? Name it, its gene and what kind of transporter it is.",
 options:[
  {t:"P-glycoprotein (P-gp); ABCB1; efflux", correct:true, why:"Her answer: PGP, encoded by ABCB1, an efflux transporter that pumps drug back into the gut lumen."},
  {t:"P-glycoprotein (P-gp); CYP2C19; uptake", correct:false, why:"CYP2C19 is a metabolizing enzyme gene, and P-gp pumps drug out, not in."},
  {t:"Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase; G6PD; efflux", correct:false, why:"G6PD is an enzyme, from the primaquine story."},
  {t:"VKORC1; ABCB1; uptake", correct:false, why:"VKORC1 is a gene named on the nomenclature slides, not a gut transporter."},
  {t:"HLA antigen; ABCB1; efflux", correct:false, why:"HLA antigens are the immune target area."}],
 teach:"P-glycoprotein (P-gp) is expressed in the gut and is encoded by the ABCB1 gene, in which many genetic variants have been defined. It is an efflux transporter: as an oral drug tries to cross the gut lumen cells, P-gp grabs it and pumps it back into the lumen to be excreted. Other transporters are expressed in the liver, brain and elsewhere, and their variants are an active research area.",
 quote:"So, can you think of any drug transporters? I'm thinking of one that's expressed in the gut, PGP P glacoprotein, right? OK. It's encoded by the ABCB1 gene, OK? ... Remember, PGP is an e-flux transporter. Right?",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Introduction: Three Target Areas’ (p. 10); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-021", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"targets", skill:"apply",
 concept:"pgp-affects-bioavailability", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"An oral drug is a substrate for P-glycoprotein in the gut. Which aspect of its kinetics does that affect?",
 options:[
  {t:"Bioavailability", correct:true, why:"Her answer: absorption into the systemic circulation, the term bioavailability."},
  {t:"Volume of distribution", correct:false, why:"Not the aspect she named for gut P-gp."},
  {t:"Plasma protein binding", correct:false, why:"Not the aspect she named."},
  {t:"Hepatic metabolism", correct:false, why:"P-gp is a transporter, not a metabolizing enzyme."},
  {t:"Renal filtration", correct:false, why:"A student offered excretion; she steered to bioavailability."}],
 teach:"For an oral drug that is a P-gp substrate, gut P-gp limits how much drug is absorbed into the systemic circulation, which is the drug's bioavailability. That is why she thinks about bioavailability whenever an oral drug is a P-gp substrate.",
 quote:"What aspect of kinetics could be affected by the fact that that drug that you're taking orally is a substrate for what aspect of kinetics does that affect? I'm thinking absorption into the systemic circulation. ... About the term bioavailability. OK. So I think about bioavailability a lot when I think about oral medications that are substrates for PGP.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Introduction: Three Target Areas’ (p. 10); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-022", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"targets", skill:"apply",
 concept:"low-pgp-digoxin-bioavailability-up", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A patient taking oral digoxin, a narrow therapeutic index drug, has a low-activity version of P-glycoprotein. What happens to digoxin's bioavailability?",
 options:[
  {t:"It goes up", correct:true, why:"Her answer: low-activity P-gp pumps less digoxin back into the lumen, more is absorbed, and blood levels are high on a normal dose."},
  {t:"It goes down", correct:false, why:"Less efflux means more absorption, not less."},
  {t:"It does not change", correct:false, why:"Digoxin's dose assumes normal P-gp activity in the gut."},
  {t:"It drops to zero", correct:false, why:"Drug still gets absorbed; more of it does."},
  {t:"It cannot be predicted from P-gp activity", correct:false, why:"She predicted it directly from the efflux role."}],
 teach:"Digoxin is a P-gp substrate in the gut, and its dose is set knowing that P-gp blocks part of its absorption. A patient with a low-activity P-gp variant pumps less digoxin back into the lumen, so bioavailability goes up and blood levels can be high on a normal dose, affecting heart rate too much. Finding such variants explained why some patients had lots of digoxin in the bloodstream; she will return to this example.",
 quote:"OK, so people that have a low activity version of PGP. What's going to happen to their bioavailability of digoxin? Does the bioavailability go up or does it go down? Who said that up, it goes up.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Introduction: Three Target Areas’ (p. 10); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-023", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"targets", skill:"recall",
 concept:"drug-targets-oncology", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Where do genetic variations in drug targets have the biggest role right now?",
 options:[
  {t:"Oncology", correct:true, why:"Her words: the biggest role right now is in oncology, along with some immunotherapy drugs."},
  {t:"Anesthesia", correct:false, why:"Not the area she named."},
  {t:"Antimalarial prophylaxis", correct:false, why:"That is the primaquine history, a metabolizing-enzyme phenotype."},
  {t:"Poison treatment", correct:false, why:"Not the area she named."},
  {t:"Vaccines", correct:false, why:"Not the area she named."}],
 teach:"A drug target is where a drug acts: a receptor or an enzyme it inhibits. Variants in drug targets explain another part of why people respond differently; the area has moved more slowly than metabolizing enzymes or transporters. An evidence-based pharmacogenomics guidance document on beta-adrenergic receptor variants was published two years ago, and oncology, where drugs are designed to hit a protein the cancer cell expresses, is where drug targets matter most now.",
 quote:"An evidence-based guidance document for pharmacogenomics published 2 years ago that deals with some of our beta-adrenergic receptors and variants in those receptors. ... The area where drug targets has the biggest role right now is in oncology.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Introduction: Three Target Areas’ (p. 10); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-024", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"targets", skill:"recall",
 concept:"immune-hla-target-area", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"What is the newest target area of pharmacogenomics, and what kind of genetic variation does it involve?",
 options:[
  {t:"Immune system; HLA (human leukocyte antigen) variants", correct:true, why:"People with certain HLA antigen variants respond badly, with severe adverse reactions, to certain drugs; the list grows every year."},
  {t:"Drug transporters; variants in the ABCB1 gene for P-glycoprotein", correct:false, why:"Transporters were the second area, not the newest."},
  {t:"Metabolizing enzymes; variants in the P450s", correct:false, why:"The first and best-known area."},
  {t:"Drug targets; variants in beta-adrenergic receptors", correct:false, why:"Drug targets were the third area."},
  {t:"The immune system; variants in G6PD", correct:false, why:"G6PD is an enzyme from the primaquine history."}],
 teach:"The latest area that pharmacogenomics is being used to explain is the immune system. People with certain variants of their HLA antigens respond badly to certain drugs, with severe adverse drug reactions. Every year the list of drugs and related HLA variants in the literature gets a little longer; details come in later lectures.",
 quote:"For example, We have some medications that people uh with certain genetic variations in their HLA antigens, they are going to respond badly to these drugs if they have certain variants of their HLA antigens, OK?",
 note:"Not on the printed slide, which lists three areas; she added immune system targets aloud that morning. HLA is expanded here as human leukocyte antigen; she said only \"HLA antigens\".",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Introduction: Three Target Areas’ (p. 10); transcript 10/20"},

/* ---------------- Impact on PK and PD ---------------- */
{id:"L17-025", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"pkpd", skill:"figure", type:"match",
 concept:"pgx-info-flow-figure", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Match each part of the PharmGKB information-flow figure with what it contains.",
 left:["Top arc: variants in PK genes", "Bottom arc: variants in PD genes", "Clinical outcome"],
 right:["Absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion", "Target, mechanism of action, drug response", "Efficacy or toxicity"],
 pairs:[
  {l:"Top arc: variants in PK genes", r:"Absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion", why:"Variants in metabolizing enzymes or transporters change the pharmacokinetics (PK) of the drug."},
  {l:"Bottom arc: variants in PD genes", r:"Target, mechanism of action, drug response", why:"A variant drug target, such as a receptor, can change the pharmacodynamics (PD) and the response."},
  {l:"Clinical outcome", r:"Efficacy or toxicity", why:"Her two buckets."}],
 teach:"The figure from the PharmGKB (Pharmacogenetics and Pharmacogenomics Knowledge Base) website splits pharmacogenomics into kinetics and dynamics. The top arc runs from variants in PK genes to absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion; the bottom arc from variants in PD genes to target, mechanism of action and drug response. Either way the end is a clinical outcome, which she sorts into efficacy or toxicity.",
 quote:"So, the top arc here. Is thinking about kinetics. And we might be looking at variants in, let's say, metabolizing enzymes or variants in transporters. ... So then the other bottom arc of this figure is again looking at variants in our genotypes.",
 note:"The transcript renders PharmGKB as \"Farm GKB\"; she said the site now routes to a new version.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Impact of Pharmacogenomics on PK and PD’ (p. 11, PharmGKB figure); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-026", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"pkpd", skill:"apply", type:"match",
 concept:"efficacy-toxicity-buckets", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Sort each genetic finding into the outcome bucket it was used to illustrate.",
 left:["A variant makes a drug-metabolizing enzyme less active", "A variant makes a drug's receptor respond poorly", "A patient's cancer does not express the protein the drug binds"],
 right:["Toxicity", "Efficacy", "Efficacy (individualized medicine)"],
 pairs:[
  {l:"A variant makes a drug-metabolizing enzyme less active", r:"Toxicity", why:"The drug is not cleared, blood concentrations rise, and the patient has an adverse response; transporters can add to this bucket too."},
  {l:"A variant makes a drug's receptor respond poorly", r:"Efficacy", why:"The drug cannot get its response from a receptor that does not work well."},
  {l:"A patient's cancer does not express the protein the drug binds", r:"Efficacy (individualized medicine)", why:"Nothing for the drug to bind: it will not work."}],
 teach:"She sorts clinical outcomes into two buckets. Toxicity: a less active metabolizing enzyme (or a transporter variant) raises drug concentrations, a PK effect. Efficacy: a drug-target variant, such as a poorly responding receptor, or a cancer that lacks the protein a targeted drug binds. Pharmacogenomics is used either to prevent toxicity or to ensure efficacy.",
 quote:"So I like to think of the outcomes as being put into the bucket of efficacy or put into the bucket of toxicity. ... So We always try to either prevent toxicity or ensure efficacy by using pharmacogenomics.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Impact of Pharmacogenomics on PK and PD’ (p. 11); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-027", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"pkpd", skill:"apply",
 concept:"cancer-lacks-target-protein", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A targeted cancer drug works by binding a specific protein. Testing shows the patient's cancer does not express that protein. What should be done?",
 options:[
  {t:"Do not give that drug", correct:true, why:"There is nothing for it to bind, so it will not work; it is expensive and can cause side effects."},
  {t:"Give it at a higher dose", correct:false, why:"A higher dose cannot make the drug work without its target."},
  {t:"Give it with a P-glycoprotein inhibitor", correct:false, why:"Absorption is not the problem; the target is missing."},
  {t:"Give it and watch for toxicity only", correct:false, why:"It will not work, and it can still cause side effects."},
  {t:"Genotype the liver enzymes first", correct:false, why:"The question is the cancer's protein, a drug-target issue."}],
 teach:"Individualized medicine designs drugs to hit a protein a cancer cell expresses. If the patient's cancer does not have that protein, the drug has nothing to bind and will not work; it is expensive and can still cause side effects, so it should not be given. This sits in the efficacy bucket.",
 quote:"If their cancer doesn't have that protein, don't give them this drug. It's expensive, and it can cause side effects.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Impact of Pharmacogenomics on PK and PD’ (p. 11); transcript 10/20"},

/* ---------------- Wild type, variant, polymorphism ---------------- */
{id:"L17-028", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"variant", skill:"term",
 concept:"wild-type-vs-variant", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What is the wild type of a gene, and what is a variant?",
 options:[
  {t:"Usually the most common version; a less common version", correct:true, why:"Her test answer: wild type is the most common version, a variant is a less common one."},
  {t:"The version in animals; the version in humans", correct:false, why:"Not the definition."},
  {t:"The disease-causing version; the healthy, normal version", correct:false, why:"Neither term means disease or health."},
  {t:"A version below 1% of people; one above 1%", correct:false, why:"The 1% cutoff separates a polymorphism from a rare variant."},
  {t:"The mother's copy; the father's copy", correct:false, why:"Those are the two alleles."}],
 teach:"Wild type is the version of a gene thought to be the most common; a variant is a version that is not the most common. Sometimes the gene called wild type in the literature is simply the one scientists understood first, so the definitions are a little fluid, but on her test \"wild type is the most common\" is accepted. The word mutant is no longer used for a variant.",
 quote:"OK. Wild type is a term used to talk about a version of a gene that is the most common, usually. ... When I test you on it, I will say, if you tell me the wild type is the most common, I'll say, yeah, that's fine, OK?",
 note:"She added that \"Some people like to use the word mutant as a word synonymous with variant, but we tend not to use the word mutant any longer\". The slide wording is \"the version of a gene variation that is thought to be the most common\".",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Wildtype vs Variant’ (p. 13); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-029", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"variant", skill:"term",
 concept:"polymorphism-vs-rare-variant", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What separates a polymorphism from a rare variant?",
 options:[
  {t:"How often it occurs: at least 1% vs less than 1%", correct:true, why:"A polymorphism is a genetic variant present in at least 1% of a population; a rare variant is present in less than 1%."},
  {t:"Its size: one nucleotide vs a whole gene", correct:false, why:"Any kind of variation can be a polymorphism; the definition is about frequency."},
  {t:"Its effect: harmless vs harmful", correct:false, why:"A rare variant can be just as dangerous for that patient."},
  {t:"Its location: coding region vs between genes", correct:false, why:"Location is not the definition."},
  {t:"How often it occurs: at least 10% vs less than 10%", correct:false, why:"The cutoff is 1%."}],
 teach:"A polymorphism is a genetic variant that occurs in at least 1% of a population; a rare variant occurs in less than 1%. The difference sounds small, but in genetics it is a big one. Most pharmacogenomics work has been on polymorphisms, so she uses that word most.",
 quote:"So, a polymorphism is a genetic variant. And by definition, a polymorphism occurs at least 1% of the time in a population, OK? ... A rare variant is a genetic variation that is occurring in less than 1% of the population.",
 note:"Wording varies: slide \"If present ≥ 1% of the population, known as a genetic polymorphism\" (the ≥ is underlined and exports as >); she says \"at least 1%\", \"greater than 1%\" and \"1% or more\". Keyed on at least 1%.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Polymorphism’ (p. 14); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-030", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"variant", skill:"apply", dupOf:"L17-029",
 concept:"gene-deletion-can-be-polymorphic", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"An entire gene is deleted in more than 1% of a population. What is that deletion called?",
 options:[
  {t:"A polymorphism", correct:true, why:"The definition speaks to frequency; any kind of variation in at least 1% of the population is polymorphic."},
  {t:"A rare variant", correct:false, why:"A rare variant occurs in less than 1%."},
  {t:"A single nucleotide polymorphism", correct:false, why:"A whole-gene deletion is not a one-nucleotide change."},
  {t:"A haplotype", correct:false, why:"A haplotype is a set of alleles at several loci on one chromosome."},
  {t:"A wild type", correct:false, why:"Wild type is the most common version."}],
 teach:"Most people think polymorphisms are just SNPs, but that is not true. A polymorphism is defined by how often a variation occurs, so a whole gene deletion, extra copies of a gene or a sister chromatid exchange that occurs in more than 1% of the population is a polymorphism. Any of the same changes in less than 1% of the population is a rare variant.",
 quote:"A whole gene deletion could be polymorphic if it's happening in more than 1% of the population. ... It's happening, polymorphism definition speaks to the frequency of that change happening, right?",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Polymorphism’ (p. 14); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-031", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"variant", skill:"apply",
 concept:"why-rare-variant-term", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Why did the term rare variant enter the vocabulary of pharmacogenomics more recently?",
 options:[
  {t:"Patients reacted badly without the known polymorphism", correct:true, why:"Closer genotyping showed an uncommon variation in fewer than 1% of people, and it still caused the bad response."},
  {t:"Polymorphisms were found to have no effect on drug response", correct:false, why:"Polymorphisms explain a lot of variation in drug response."},
  {t:"Regulators banned the word polymorphism", correct:false, why:"She still uses polymorphism most."},
  {t:"Rare variants are easier to genotype", correct:false, why:"Not the reason given."},
  {t:"It is the new name for the wild type", correct:false, why:"The wild type is the most common version."}],
 teach:"Polymorphisms (at least 1% of a population) have been characterized for a long time and explain much of the variation in drug response. As that work matured, some patients were found to respond badly without carrying the known polymorphism; a closer look showed a variation present in less than 1% of people. Those patients still feel bad or are endangered by the drug, so rare variants of any kind now get attention.",
 quote:"That's a person with a rare variant making them respond badly, so. The rare variant term has popped into our dictionary more recently, as we've made progress on understanding.",
 note:"She also asked the class to say \"genetic variant\" rather than \"aberration\".",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Polymorphism’ (p. 14); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-032", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"variant", skill:"term",
 concept:"snp-definition", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What is a single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP, pronounced snip)?",
 options:[
  {t:"One nucleotide differs from the wild type sequence", correct:true, why:"Just one nucleotide is different in the gene sequence; the slide calls it the most common type of polymorphism."},
  {t:"An entire gene is deleted from one chromosome", correct:false, why:"A deletion is another kind of polymorphism."},
  {t:"Extra copies of a gene are present", correct:false, why:"Copy number is another kind."},
  {t:"A set of alleles that travel together", correct:false, why:"That is a haplotype."},
  {t:"One nucleotide differs, in fewer than 1% of the population", correct:false, why:"A one-nucleotide change in fewer than 1% would be a rare variant, not a polymorphism."}],
 teach:"A single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP, pronounced snip) means one nucleotide in a gene is different in a patient with the polymorphism compared with a patient who has the wild type version. It is the most common type of polymorphism. Like any polymorphism it occurs in at least 1% of a population.",
 quote:"But probably a type of polymorphism that you guys have already heard about is a single nucleotide polymorphism or called a snip. ... OK, that means just one nucleotide is different in this gene sequence for your patient with a polymorphism compared to your patient without that polymorphism with the wild type version.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Polymorphism’ (p. 14); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-033", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"variant", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"kinds-of-polymorphism", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which kinds of genetic variation can be polymorphisms, provided they occur in at least 1% of a population? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"A single nucleotide change", correct:true, why:"The SNP, the most common type."},
  {t:"More than one nucleotide change", correct:true, why:"On the slide's last bullet."},
  {t:"An entire gene insertion or deletion", correct:true, why:"On the slide; a whole gene deletion can be polymorphic."},
  {t:"Extra copies of a gene", correct:true, why:"On the slide."},
  {t:"A sister chromatid exchange", correct:true, why:"Named aloud: if it happens in more than 1% of the population, it is a polymorphism."},
  {t:"Only single nucleotide changes qualify", correct:false, why:"\"polymorphisms, I think most people feel are just snips. That's not true.\""}],
 teach:"SNPs are the easiest polymorphisms to talk about and the most common, but other kinds of variation are also polymorphisms: more than one nucleotide change, an entire gene insertion or deletion, extra copies of a gene, a sister chromatid exchange, and more. The label depends only on how often the variation occurs.",
 quote:"But there are other kinds of genetic variations that are also called polymorphisms, and they can include um more than one nucleotide change or an entire gene insertion, or an entire gene deletion or extra copies of a gene, and more. So, polymorphisms, I think most people feel are just snips. That's not true.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Polymorphism’ (p. 14); transcript 10/20"},

/* ---------------- Allele; homozygosity and heterozygosity ---------------- */
{id:"L17-034", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"allele", skill:"term",
 concept:"allele-definition", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What is an allele?",
 options:[
  {t:"One of a person's two copies of a gene, one from each parent", correct:true, why:"Humans are diploid; one copy is an allele and the other copy is an allele, one from the biological mother and one from the biological father."},
  {t:"A set of variant alleles that occur together on one chromosome", correct:false, why:"That is a haplotype."},
  {t:"The protein a gene produces", correct:false, why:"That is the gene product."},
  {t:"A variant found in fewer than 1% of people", correct:false, why:"That is a rare variant."},
  {t:"The observable trait a gene produces", correct:false, why:"That is a phenotype."}],
 teach:"Humans are diploid: we generally have two copies of every chromosome and so two copies of the same gene, one from the biological mother and one from the biological father. Each copy is an allele. The slide gives the National Human Genome Research Institute definition: the variant and wild type forms of a gene at a particular location on a chromosome.",
 quote:"We have, most of us have two copies of every gene. And so one copy is an allele and the other copy's in allele. ... All right, so, get a little from your mother, get a little from your father. Got 2 copies.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Allele’ (p. 15); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-035", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"allele", skill:"figure",
 concept:"homozygous-heterozygous-figure", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In the homozygosity figure, the orange chromosome has big B at the third locus and the blue chromosome has little b. Is this person homozygous or heterozygous at that locus?",
 options:[
  {t:"Heterozygous for B", correct:true, why:"Her answer: the two alleles differ at that locus."},
  {t:"Homozygous for B", correct:false, why:"Homozygous means both copies are the same, as at the P locus (PP)."},
  {t:"Homozygous for b", correct:false, why:"Only one copy is little b."},
  {t:"Hemizygous for B", correct:false, why:"Not a term from the figure."},
  {t:"It cannot be said without the phenotype", correct:false, why:"Zygosity is read from the two alleles."}],
 teach:"At each locus, compare the two alleles. PP (both big P) is homozygous; aa (both little a) is homozygous; Bb (big B on one chromosome, little b on the other) is heterozygous. The figure also labels dominant and recessive alleles, but pharmacogenomics does not discuss variants as dominant or recessive; the terms to know are homozygous and heterozygous, said as \"heterozygous for B at this third locus\".",
 quote:"On their orange allele, they got Big B. And on the blue a little they got little B, so they are what, homozygous or heterozygous. They're heterozygous, right?",
 note:"The transcript renders loci as \"low side\", \"low sci\", \"low psi\". She said of dominance: \"in the world of pharmacogenomics, we don't have. Genetic variations that we discuss as being dominant versus recessive\".",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Homozygosity and Heterozygosity’ (p. 16, Addison Wesley Longman figure); transcript 10/20"},

/* ---------------- Genotype and phenotype ---------------- */
{id:"L17-036", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"genophen", skill:"term",
 concept:"genotype-vs-phenotype", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What is a genotype, and what is a phenotype?",
 options:[
  {t:"The two alleles a person has; an observable characteristic", correct:true, why:"Genotype is the pair of alleles; phenotype is any observable characteristic, which results from expression of genes."},
  {t:"An observable characteristic; the two alleles a person has", correct:false, why:"Reversed."},
  {t:"One allele; the protein it makes", correct:false, why:"A genotype is both alleles."},
  {t:"The whole genome; the drug's effect", correct:false, why:"Genotype here is the two alleles of one gene."},
  {t:"A haplotype; a polymorphism", correct:false, why:"Different terms."}],
 teach:"Each person carries two alleles of each gene, and those two alleles are the genotype. A phenotype is any observable characteristic of an organism, morphological, developmental, biochemical, physical or behavioral, and it results from the expression of genes: the genotype drives the phenotype. In pharmacogenomics a phenotype is often an enzyme's activity, such as how active a person's CYP2C19 is.",
 quote:"The two alleles that anybody has represents their genotype, right? ... Phenotypes are driven by the genotype, OK?",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Genotype vs phenotype’ (p. 17); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-037", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"genophen", skill:"apply",
 concept:"writing-genotype-cyp2c19", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"How is the genotype written for a person homozygous for the *2 (star 2) allele of CYP2C19 (cytochrome P450 2C19)?",
 options:[
  {t:"CYP2C19*2/*2", correct:true, why:"The gene, then the allele on each copy: both copies are *2."},
  {t:"CYP2C19*2", correct:false, why:"That names the allele, not the person's two copies."},
  {t:"CYP2C19*1/*2", correct:false, why:"That is a person with one wild type and one *2 copy."},
  {t:"CYP2C19*4", correct:false, why:"The two star numbers are not added."},
  {t:"2C19-*2 homozygous", correct:false, why:"Not the written form."}],
 teach:"The written genotype gives the gene, then the allele on each of the two copies separated by a slash. A person homozygous for the *2 allele of CYP2C19 is written CYP2C19*2/*2. CYP2C19 is a P450 that does a lot of drug metabolism and has many genetic variations; it will come up again and again.",
 quote:"So if somebody were homozygous for SIP 2C192, we could write it like this, a little code going on here. So CIP2C192, 2. I've given you the gene. I've told you what allele they have. Both their allele copies are the same, they're homozygous for 2.",
 note:"The transcript renders CYP2C19*2 as \"SIP 2C192\", \"CIP2C192\", \"STC 192\" and star as \"SAR\" and \"SA\". The slide prints \"Genotype = CYP2C19*2/*2\". Star-allele nomenclature itself (pp. 25–28) was not reached; see L17-055 to L17-057.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Genotype vs phenotype’ (p. 17); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-038", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"genophen", skill:"apply",
 concept:"cyp2c19-star2-no-activity", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"What is the phenotype of a person whose genotype is CYP2C19*2/*2?",
 options:[
  {t:"No CYP2C19 enzyme activity", correct:true, why:"The *2 variant has no activity; the person cannot metabolize drugs through 2C19."},
  {t:"Normal CYP2C19 activity", correct:false, why:"Normal activity belongs to the wild type."},
  {t:"Extra CYP2C19 activity", correct:false, why:"*2 has no activity."},
  {t:"Half the normal activity", correct:false, why:"Not what she said for a person homozygous for *2."},
  {t:"Unknown; the relationship is unclear", correct:false, why:"She called the genotype-phenotype relationship for *2 \"really tight\"."}],
 teach:"The CYP2C19 *2 variant has no enzyme activity, so a person who is CYP2C19*2/*2 has no 2C19 activity and cannot metabolize drugs through that enzyme; that is the phenotype. There are good data for this: the genotype-phenotype relationship for *2 is tight, and even the reason the enzyme is not active is known.",
 quote:"Well, I will tell you that the STC 192 variant has No activity. OK. So that's the phenotype. ... So I would say that the knowledge of the phenotype genotype relationship for 2C192 is really tight.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Genotype vs phenotype’ (p. 17); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-039", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"genophen", skill:"term",
 concept:"functional-significance", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"What is the functional significance of a genetic variation? (She asked the room to explain it as they would to a grandmother.)",
 options:[
  {t:"How the change affects the function of the gene's protein", correct:true, why:"Her answer: how the mutation affects the protein, enzyme or transporter that the gene creates."},
  {t:"How often the change occurs in a population", correct:false, why:"That is the frequency, which separates polymorphisms from rare variants."},
  {t:"Which parent passed the change on", correct:false, why:"Not the definition."},
  {t:"Where the change sits on the chromosome", correct:false, why:"That is the locus."},
  {t:"Whether the change is dominant or recessive in Mendelian terms", correct:false, why:"Dominance is not used in pharmacogenomics."}],
 teach:"Functional significance is the key phrase of the topic: what a change in a gene does to how that gene's product, a protein, enzyme or transporter, works. Without it, a drug response phenotype cannot be explained. Examples with known functional significance are the ones that can be used to decide whether a patient gets a drug or a reduced dose.",
 quote:"So, what's the keyword I mentioned earlier when we got on this slide? I said, functional significance. What's the functional significance of a genetic variation? How would you explain that to your grandmother? ... How does that mutation affect the function of the protein, or the enzyme or the transporter that that gene creates in our body, OK? Why do we need to know the functional significance? Well, if we don't know how that genetic variation is changing how the gene product works, how are we gonna explain our drug response phenotype?",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Complications of Genotype vs phenotype’ (p. 18); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-040", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"genophen", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"genotype-phenotype-complications", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which of the following complicate the genotype-phenotype relationship? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Many polymorphisms may be present in a single gene", correct:true, why:"The first bullet."},
  {t:"Most traits are multigenic, not monogenic", correct:true, why:"The second bullet; drug responses can be multigenic too."},
  {t:"Environmental factors influence gene expression", correct:true, why:"The third bullet: gene-environment interactions."},
  {t:"Most traits are monogenic", correct:false, why:"The reverse of the slide."},
  {t:"Wild type is always the most common version", correct:false, why:"Not a complication on the slide, and not always true."}],
 teach:"Three things complicate the picture. One gene can carry many polymorphisms, so it is hard to tell which variation causes the phenotype. Most traits, and many drug responses, are multigenic rather than monogenic. And environmental factors, including epigenetic factors, influence how genes are expressed.",
 quote:"One of them is that many polymorphisms could be present in one gene. ... All right, how about the next bullet? Most traits are multigenic, not monogenic. ... And also another thing that complicates our phenotype genotype relationship is the fact that you've got environmental factors that influence.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Complications of Genotype vs phenotype’ (p. 18); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-041", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"genophen", skill:"apply",
 concept:"many-polymorphisms-one-gene", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Why does having many polymorphisms in one gene complicate the genotype-phenotype relationship?",
 options:[
  {t:"It is hard to tell which variation causes the phenotype", correct:true, why:"The room's answer, accepted: with several variable places in a gene, it takes a lot of work to find which one drives the response."},
  {t:"Genes with many polymorphisms cannot be sequenced accurately", correct:false, why:"The variations are identified; assigning the effect is the hard part."},
  {t:"Each polymorphism doubles the enzyme's activity", correct:false, why:"Not a rule she gave."},
  {t:"Such genes are always rare variants", correct:false, why:"Frequency is a separate question."},
  {t:"The gene then has no wild type", correct:false, why:"Not the reason given."}],
 teach:"A single gene can have several places that vary: a SNP here, a tandem repeat there. The variations can be identified, but it takes a lot of work to figure out which one is responsible for the way a patient responds, and sometimes it depends on the structure of the gene.",
 quote:"Why would it complicate our understanding of what's the phenotype you get with those genotypes? Why does that complicate it? It's hard to figure out which genetic variation is causing the phenotype.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Complications of Genotype vs phenotype’ (p. 18); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-042", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"genophen", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"monogenic-disease-examples", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Which diseases were offered as examples caused by one genetic variation? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Sickle cell disease", correct:true, why:"Offered by the room and accepted: \"some sickle cell, yes\"."},
  {t:"Huntington's chorea", correct:true, why:"Her example, caused by one genetic variant in one allele."},
  {t:"Tay-Sachs disease", correct:true, why:"Her example, a metabolic disorder."},
  {t:"Diabetes", correct:false, why:"Named as multigenic."},
  {t:"Cardiovascular disease", correct:false, why:"Named as multigenic."},
  {t:"Hyperlipidemia", correct:false, why:"Named as multigenic."}],
 teach:"A few diseases are caused by one genetic variation: sickle cell, Huntington's chorea, certain kinds of early-onset Alzheimer's and Parkinson's, and Tay-Sachs disease. These are fairly rare. Most diseases, such as cardiovascular disease, diabetes and hyperlipidemia, are multigenic, and the same holds for drug response: some responses follow one variant, but many involve more than one.",
 quote:"Any diseases come to mind that are caused by one gene mutation. Not usually, sorry, good, good guess. Some sickle cell, yes, some sickle cell, yes. ... Tay-Sachs disease, one genetic variation, OK? That's a metabolic problem. ... Most diseases that we get are caused by multiple changes in our genetics, right? Cardiovascular disease, diabetes, hyperlipidemia, oh, go on and on, OK?",
 note:"The transcript renders Huntington's chorea as \"Huntington's chore\" and \"Huntington's career\"; she described it as a very specific, early-onset dementia that is very inheritable.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Complications of Genotype vs phenotype’ (p. 18); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-043", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"genophen", skill:"apply",
 concept:"multigenic-drug-response-weighting", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Drug response can be multigenic. How is that handled as the science gets more sophisticated?",
 options:[
  {t:"Each gene's impact is weighted and the genotypes combined", correct:true, why:"One variant may have a large effect and another a small one; together they can predict an adverse response."},
  {t:"Only the single gene with the largest effect is ever considered", correct:false, why:"She described adding the genes' effects together."},
  {t:"Multigenic responses cannot be studied", correct:false, why:"She said the field is getting better at it."},
  {t:"Each gene must be monogenic for its effect to count", correct:false, why:"Not what she said."},
  {t:"Environmental factors are ignored in the weighting", correct:false, why:"Environment is a separate complication; she did not say it is ignored."}],
 teach:"Some drug responses follow one variant, but many involve more than one gene. The field can now sometimes weight each variant's contribution: one gene's variant has a large impact, another's a smaller one, and the two genotypes added together predict an adverse response. Several drugs later in the course involve more than one gene.",
 quote:"So sometimes we have a gene with a genetic variation, and it has this much impact on your patient's response to a drug, but this other gene also has a genetic variation that impacts their response to a drug, but maybe it only has this much effect. But we look at them together and say, oh, this genotype and this genotype added together result in adverse response.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Complications of Genotype vs phenotype’ (p. 18); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-044", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"genophen", skill:"recall",
 concept:"epigenetic-environment-factors", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Which of these are the internal factors she called epigenetic factors that affect how genes are expressed?",
 options:[
  {t:"DNA methylation and histones", correct:true, why:"Her two named epigenetic factors."},
  {t:"Diet and exercise", correct:false, why:"Environmental factors she named, but not the ones she called epigenetic."},
  {t:"Sleep and stress", correct:false, why:"Environmental factors she named, but not the ones she called epigenetic."},
  {t:"SNPs and gene deletions", correct:false, why:"Those are changes in the DNA sequence itself."},
  {t:"Allele and genotype frequency", correct:false, why:"Population measures, not expression factors."}],
 teach:"Environmental factors such as diet, habits, lack of exercise, sleep and stress can affect how genes are expressed. Some influences are more internal: the methylation of DNA and histones, which are called epigenetic factors. Both change the significance of a genetic variation and so complicate the genotype-phenotype relationship; more is known about them every year.",
 quote:"So, your diet, your habits, your lack of exercise, your sleep, your stress, all that could be affecting how your genes get expressed. Some of those are a little more internal things like our histones and our methylation of our DNA. Those are called epigenetic factors.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Complications of Genotype vs phenotype’ (p. 18); transcript 10/20"},

/* ---------------- Haplotype ---------------- */
{id:"L17-045", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"haplo", skill:"term",
 concept:"haplotype-definition", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What is a haplotype?",
 options:[
  {t:"A set of alleles at multiple loci on the same chromosome", correct:true, why:"The official definition on the slide, read aloud."},
  {t:"The pair of alleles of one gene that a single person carries", correct:false, why:"That is a genotype."},
  {t:"A single nucleotide change at one locus", correct:false, why:"That is a SNP."},
  {t:"A variant present in fewer than 1% of people", correct:false, why:"That is a rare variant."},
  {t:"One of the two copies of a gene", correct:false, why:"That is an allele."}],
 teach:"A haplotype is a set of alleles at multiple loci, or areas, of a gene that coexist on the same chromosome. It also has a statistical meaning: the set occurs together at a frequency greater than random chance. Haplotypes that affect drug response will come up later, especially with warfarin.",
 quote:"All right, haplotype, it just means it's a set of alleles at multiple loci that coexist in the same chromosome.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Haplotype’ (p. 19); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-046", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"haplo", skill:"recall",
 concept:"haplotype-greater-than-chance-ld", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"When a set of alleles co-exists at a higher frequency than random chance would predict, what is that called?",
 options:[
  {t:"Linkage disequilibrium", correct:true, why:"The slide's term; she put it as the haplotype's statistical significance, occurring more often than random chance."},
  {t:"Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium", correct:false, why:"That is about allele and genotype frequencies staying constant across generations."},
  {t:"Genotype frequency", correct:false, why:"That is how often a genotype occurs in a population."},
  {t:"Heterozygosity", correct:false, why:"That is two different alleles at one locus."},
  {t:"Rare variant", correct:false, why:"That is a variant in less than 1% of a population."}],
 teach:"A haplotype has statistical significance: its alleles occur together more often than random chance would predict. The slide names that excess linkage disequilibrium. She explained that the chemistry of a stretch of DNA can make certain loci change together in particular patterns.",
 quote:"Also, I want to say on this slide, a haplotype has a statistical significance in the world of pharmacogenemics. So, a haplotype is going to be occurring at a frequency that is greater than random chance.",
 note:"She did not say the words \"linkage disequilibrium\"; the term is on the slide she taught (p. 19), and she said every slide with a term on it is testable. She attributes the greater-than-chance frequency to the haplotype itself; the slide attaches it to linkage disequilibrium. Both kept.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Haplotype’ (p. 19); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-047", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"haplo", skill:"figure",
 concept:"haplotype-figure-agt", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"The haplotype illustration shows six people's sequences with SNPs at three loci (A/G, G/T, A/T). Which haplotypes does it identify?",
 options:[
  {t:"AGT, GTA and AGA", correct:true, why:"The orange person has AGT, the dark blue GTA, the light blue AGA."},
  {t:"AAA, GGG and TTT", correct:false, why:"No sequence shows the same base at all three loci."},
  {t:"AG, GT and AT", correct:false, why:"Those are the SNP alternatives at each locus, not the haplotypes."},
  {t:"Only AGT", correct:false, why:"Three patterns appear."},
  {t:"GAT, TGA and GAA", correct:false, why:"Not the patterns read from the figure."}],
 teach:"In the figure, three loci each carry a SNP: A or G at the first, G or T at the second, A or T at the third. Read across each person, the variants come in three combinations: AGT, GTA and AGA. Those patterns appear together more often than random chance, and each one is a haplotype.",
 quote:"The first person who's orange has AGT, OK? The third person on the bottom who's dark blue has a GTA. Then the light blue person in the middle has an AGA. ... And those three patterns of those loci changing happen more often than random chance. That would be a haplotype.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Illustration of Haplotype’ (p. 20); transcript 10/20"},

/* ---------------- Frequencies; Hardy-Weinberg ---------------- */
{id:"L17-048", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"freq", skill:"term",
 concept:"allele-vs-genotype-frequency", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"How does allele frequency differ from genotype frequency?",
 options:[
  {t:"One allele's frequency vs one genotype's frequency", correct:true, why:"Allele frequency is the relative frequency of an allele in a population; genotype frequency is that of a genotype, the two alleles together."},
  {t:"Frequency in one person vs frequency within one family", correct:false, why:"Both are population measures."},
  {t:"A percentage vs a raw count", correct:false, why:"Both are usually expressed as a proportion or percentage."},
  {t:"Wild type only vs variants only", correct:false, why:"Both wild type and variant alleles have frequencies."},
  {t:"Before vs after evolutionary pressure", correct:false, why:"That is the Hardy-Weinberg question."}],
 teach:"Allele frequency is the relative frequency of an allele in a population; genotype frequency is the relative frequency of a genotype. Both are usually expressed as a proportion or percentage. Because alleles make up a genotype, the allele frequencies of the wild type and the variant together give the genotype frequencies.",
 quote:"But if we're talking about an allele, how often does this version of an allele happen in the population that would be the allele frequency. ... You've got your allele frequency of the wild type, you've got your allele frequency of the variant allele in the population. Together, what's the genotype frequency?",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Frequencies in Pharmacogenomics’ (p. 21); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-049", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"freq", skill:"term",
 concept:"hardy-weinberg-definition", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What is Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium?",
 options:[
  {t:"Allele and genotype frequencies stay constant across generations", correct:true, why:"The slide: constant from generation to generation unless specific influences are introduced."},
  {t:"Each allele has a frequency of exactly 50%", correct:false, why:"Not the definition."},
  {t:"Alleles at several loci travel together more often than by chance", correct:false, why:"That is linkage disequilibrium."},
  {t:"Identical twins have identical drug half-lives", correct:false, why:"That is the twin study finding."},
  {t:"A variant occurs in at least 1% of a population", correct:false, why:"That is the polymorphism definition."}],
 teach:"Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium means both the allele and genotype frequencies in a population remain constant from generation to generation unless specific influences are introduced. In her words, it is a special statistical analysis that asks whether a genetic variation seen in a population is there because of evolutionary pressure or not.",
 quote:"It's a very special kind of statistical analysis that we do in genetics, OK? And basically what it's saying is, when you see a population of people and you identify a genetic variation, is that genetic variation. Occurring because of evolutionary pressure or not, OK.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Frequencies in Pharmacogenomics’ (p. 21); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-050", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"freq", skill:"apply",
 concept:"hwe-stress-scenario", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"In her example, a study of this class finds a different drug response frequency than a study of all the school's past graduates, and something (she suggested stress) has pressured the class. What does that mean for the class data?",
 options:[
  {t:"Not in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium; not generalizable", correct:true, why:"Something pressured the class to respond differently, so its results cannot be applied to the general population."},
  {t:"In Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium; generalizable to everyone", correct:false, why:"A pressured population is out of equilibrium."},
  {t:"Linkage disequilibrium; a new haplotype", correct:false, why:"Haplotypes are about alleles at several loci together."},
  {t:"A rare variant; publish it as new", correct:false, why:"She said such data would not be used or published."},
  {t:"Polymorphic; the cutoff is 1%", correct:false, why:"The issue is the sample, not the variant's frequency."}],
 teach:"If a sampled group responds differently from the population it is meant to represent because some pressure has changed it, the sample is not in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium. Its results cannot be generalized to everyone, and new subjects without that pressure would have to be found. She described it as evolutionary pressure: the population has changed compared with the general population.",
 quote:"And we can't use this data because we are not in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium. Something has pressured you guys to respond differently to the drug than all the other FSOP graduates that I studied.",
 note:"FSOP in the quote is her pharmacy school. She stressed that the stress premise was hypothetical.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Frequencies in Pharmacogenomics’ (p. 21); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-051", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"freq", skill:"apply", dupOf:"L17-050",
 concept:"hwe-in-papers", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Why do authors of pharmacogenomic studies state that their study population was in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium?",
 options:[
  {t:"It shows the sample is good and generalizable", correct:true, why:"Without equilibrium the results probably cannot be extended to the world, and the paper would probably not be published."},
  {t:"It proves every subject carried the variant", correct:false, why:"It is about the population's frequencies, not each subject."},
  {t:"It shows the drug was effective", correct:false, why:"Efficacy is a separate outcome."},
  {t:"It is required only for twin studies", correct:false, why:"She said everybody does the calculation in pharmacogenomic studies."},
  {t:"It shows the alleles are in linkage disequilibrium", correct:false, why:"A different concept."}],
 teach:"Every pharmacogenomic study does the Hardy-Weinberg calculation. Papers report that the study population was in equilibrium to show that the sample was good and the results are generalizable. If the population is not in equilibrium, the results probably cannot be spread to the world, and the paper would probably not be published.",
 quote:"So when you see papers, you will be reading some papers about pharmacogenomic studies, you will always see the authors say, my study population was in Hardy-Winberg equilibrium. I pulled a great sample, it's generalizable. ... but if you don't have equilibrium, then you probably can't spread your results out to the world.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Frequencies in Pharmacogenomics’ (p. 21); transcript 10/20"},

/* ---------------- Objectives and exam scope ---------------- */
{id:"L17-052", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"scope", skill:"recall",
 concept:"every-term-on-a-slide", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"The objective under pharmacogenomics terminology is to define the terminology. Which terms does that cover?",
 options:[
  {t:"Every term on a slide she taught", correct:true, why:"\"Every slide with a term on it, you need to know those terms\"; she does not list each one."},
  {t:"Only terms written out in the objectives", correct:false, why:"She said there is no sense writing out define allele, define haplotype."},
  {t:"Only allele and haplotype", correct:false, why:"Those were her examples, not the limit."},
  {t:"Only terms from the readings", correct:false, why:"The slides and her words set the terms."},
  {t:"None; terminology is not tested", correct:false, why:"It is an objective."}],
 teach:"The objectives slide lists few objectives, but each can hold a lot of detail. Under pharmacogenomics terminology the objective is to define the terminology she talks about: every slide with a term on it is fair game, without each term being written out as its own objective.",
 quote:"So, for example, under pharmacogenomics terminology, I say define the terminology that I talk about. Every slide with a term on it, you need to know those terms, OK? There's no sense in me writing out define allele, define haplotype, OK?",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Outline and Objectives’ (p. 2); transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L17-053", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"scope", skill:"recall",
 concept:"exam3-vote-includes-pgx-day1", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"At the end of the 10/20 session the class voted on the scope of Friday's exam. What was decided?",
 options:[
  {t:"Toxicology plus the 10/20 pharmacogenomics material", correct:true, why:"The class voted for the exam to cover everything through what was done that day; she said that matches the syllabus."},
  {t:"Toxicology only, with pharmacogenomics moved to the next exam", correct:false, why:"That was the other choice; it lost."},
  {t:"All pharmacogenomics through the nomenclature lecture of 10/21", correct:false, why:"Nomenclature starts 10/21 and was not part of the choice."},
  {t:"Pharmacogenomics only, with toxicology moved to the final", correct:false, why:"Toxicology, including the poisoning table, is on the exam either way."},
  {t:"Toxicology without the poisoning table", correct:false, why:"She said the poisoning table is included either way."}],
 teach:"She offered two choices: the exam covers only toxicology, or it covers everything through what was done on 10/20. The poisoning table is on the exam either way. The class voted for the second, which is how the syllabus has it; polymorphism nomenclature starts 10/21 and is outside Friday's exam.",
 quote:"You have the choice of your exam covers only toxicology, or your exam covers everything through what we got done today. ... Who wants to have your exam cover this stuff that we covered in pharmacogenomics today? All right, that's what I do. That's what we'll do. That's how we have it on the syllabus.",
 note:"She had argued for including the material: \"we haven't gone through too much totally new stuff in today's lecture ... When we start tomorrow on polymorphism nomenclature, that's when it gets a little crazy.\" The material taught on 10/20 runs through the ‘Frequencies in Pharmacogenomics’ slide (PDF p. 21).",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Outline and Objectives’ (p. 2); transcript 10/20"},

/* ---------------- Polymorphism nomenclature (not reached on 10/20) ---------------- */
{id:"L17-054", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"nomen", skill:"recall", lowYield:true,
 concept:"snp-nomenclature-letters", tags:["smith"], source:"slide",
 stem:"In the SNP nomenclature VKORC1 1173 C>T, what do 1173, C and T stand for?",
 options:[
  {t:"Nucleotide position; original base; changed base", correct:true, why:"Numbers after the gene give the nucleotide's location; the first letter is the original (wild type) nucleotide, the second the nucleotide that changed."},
  {t:"Nucleotide position; changed base; original base", correct:false, why:"The original base comes first."},
  {t:"Amino acid number; original amino acid; new amino acid", correct:false, why:"C and T are nucleotides."},
  {t:"Year described; chromosome; tissue", correct:false, why:"Not the nomenclature."},
  {t:"Star allele number; mother's base; father's base", correct:false, why:"Star alleles are written with an asterisk."}],
 teach:"In SNP nomenclature the first letters and numbers identify the gene (VKORC1, ABCB1), the number after the gene gives the location of the nucleotide (1173, 3435), the first letter is the original or wild type nucleotide, and the letter after the > is the nucleotide that changed to make the SNP.",
 quote:"When we start tomorrow on polymorphism nomenclature, that's when it gets a little crazy.",
 note:"Low yield: the nomenclature slides (PDF pp. 22–29) were not reached on 10/20; she said polymorphism nomenclature starts 10/21, and by the class vote Friday's exam covers only what was done on 10/20. Slide content only; replace with her words once taught.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘SNP Nomenclature’ (p. 24); not reached on 10/20 (transcript 10/20)"},

{id:"L17-055", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"nomen", skill:"recall", lowYield:true,
 concept:"star-allele-nomenclature", tags:["smith"], source:"slide",
 stem:"In CYP2C19*2, what does the *2 designate, and which nucleotide change does it stand for?",
 options:[
  {t:"The allele; CYP2C19 681 G>A", correct:true, why:"The star and number after the gene designate the allele; *2 is listed as 681 G>A."},
  {t:"The allele; CYP2C19 636 G>A", correct:false, why:"636 G>A is *3."},
  {t:"The second exon; CYP2C19 681 G>A", correct:false, why:"The star designates an allele, not an exon."},
  {t:"Two copies of the gene; no change", correct:false, why:"Copy number is written differently (for example CYP2D6*2XN)."},
  {t:"The wild type; no change", correct:false, why:"*1 is the wild type."}],
 teach:"Star nomenclature names the gene, then a star and a number for the allele. For CYP2C19, *1 is the wild type, *2 is 681 G>A and *3 is 636 G>A. The relevance: CYP2C19 function varies with the allele.",
 quote:"One of those genetic variations is called the SAR 2 allele.",
 note:"Low yield: the star-nomenclature slides (PDF pp. 25–26) were not reached on 10/20; the *2 allele appeared only in the genotype example (L17-037, L17-038). Outside Friday's exam by the class vote.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Allele “Star” Nomenclature’ (p. 25); not reached on 10/20 (transcript 10/20)"},

{id:"L17-056", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"nomen", skill:"tell", lowYield:true,
 concept:"same-star-different-function", tags:["smith"], source:"slide",
 stem:"CYP2C19*2 and CYP2C9*2 both carry the *2 label. How do their effects on enzyme activity compare?",
 options:[
  {t:"CYP2C19*2: none; CYP2C9*2: decreased", correct:true, why:"Same-looking nomenclature, different functional effects, depending on the protein."},
  {t:"Both have no activity", correct:false, why:"CYP2C9*2 is decreased, not absent."},
  {t:"Both have decreased activity", correct:false, why:"CYP2C19*2 has no activity."},
  {t:"CYP2C19*2: decreased; CYP2C9*2: none", correct:false, why:"Reversed."},
  {t:"Both have normal activity", correct:false, why:"Normal activity is *1 for both."}],
 teach:"For CYP2C19, *1 is normal (wild type) activity and *2 and *3 have no activity. For CYP2C9, *1 is normal and *2 and *3 have decreased activity. Key point: allele nomenclature can look exactly the same but have different functional effects depending on the specific protein.",
 quote:"Well, I will tell you that the STC 192 variant has No activity.",
 note:"Low yield: slide p. 26 was not reached on 10/20; only the CYP2C19*2 half (no activity) was spoken, on the genotype slide. Outside Friday's exam by the class vote.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘More Allele “Star” Nomenclature’ (p. 26); not reached on 10/20 (transcript 10/20)"},

{id:"L17-057", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"nomen", skill:"apply", type:"match", lowYield:true,
 concept:"cyp2c19-genotype-activity", tags:["smith"], source:"slide",
 stem:"Match each CYP2C19 genotype with its enzyme activity.",
 left:["CYP2C19*1/*1", "CYP2C19*1/*2", "CYP2C19*2/*3"],
 right:["Normal (wild type) activity", "Reduced activity", "No activity"],
 pairs:[
  {l:"CYP2C19*1/*1", r:"Normal (wild type) activity", why:"Two wild type alleles."},
  {l:"CYP2C19*1/*2", r:"Reduced activity", why:"One working and one non-working allele; *1/*3 is the same."},
  {l:"CYP2C19*2/*3", r:"No activity", why:"Two non-working alleles; so are *2/*2 and *3/*3."}],
 teach:"Genotype nomenclature writes both alleles. For CYP2C19: *1/*1 is normal (wild type) activity; *1/*2 or *1/*3 is reduced activity; *2/*2, *2/*3 and *3/*3 have no enzyme activity.",
 quote:"When we start tomorrow on polymorphism nomenclature, that's when it gets a little crazy.",
 note:"Low yield: the ‘Genotype Nomenclature’ slide (p. 28) was not reached on 10/20. The *2/*2 row (no activity) was taught on the genotype slide (L17-038). Outside Friday's exam by the class vote.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Genotype Nomenclature’ (p. 28); not reached on 10/20 (transcript 10/20)"},

/* ---------------- Types of genetic variants (not reached on 10/20) ---------------- */
{id:"L17-058", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"types", skill:"recall", type:"match", lowYield:true,
 concept:"variant-types-list", tags:["smith"], source:"slide",
 stem:"Match each type of genetic variant with its description.",
 left:["Insertion-deletion polymorphism", "Tandem repeat", "Frameshift mutation", "Premature stop codon polymorphism", "Copy number variant"],
 right:["A nucleotide or sequence is added to or deleted from the DNA", "A nucleotide sequence repeats one after another", "An insertion or deletion not a multiple of 3 disrupts the reading frame", "A stop codon ends the polypeptide chain early", "Whole genes or segments over 1 kb are duplicated, deleted or rearranged"],
 pairs:[
  {l:"Insertion-deletion polymorphism", r:"A nucleotide or sequence is added to or deleted from the DNA", why:"Item 2 of the list."},
  {l:"Tandem repeat", r:"A nucleotide sequence repeats one after another", why:"Item 3; the slide's example is a five-tandem repeat."},
  {l:"Frameshift mutation", r:"An insertion or deletion not a multiple of 3 disrupts the reading frame", why:"Item 4."},
  {l:"Premature stop codon polymorphism", r:"A stop codon ends the polypeptide chain early", why:"Item 7."},
  {l:"Copy number variant", r:"Whole genes or segments over 1 kb are duplicated, deleted or rearranged", why:"Item 8."}],
 teach:"The slide lists eight types: single nucleotide polymorphism; insertion-deletion polymorphism; tandem repeats; frameshift mutation (an insertion or deletion whose length is not a multiple of 3); defective splicing; aberrant splice site; premature stop codon; and copy number variants (genes or segments over 1 kb duplicated, deleted or rearranged).",
 quote:"We might not get through the different types of polymorphisms today, but that's OK.",
 note:"Low yield: the types-of-variants slides (PDF pp. 30–41) were not reached on 10/20, as she predicted at the start. Outside Friday's exam by the class vote.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘List of genetic variant types’ (p. 31); not reached on 10/20 (transcript 10/20)"},

{id:"L17-059", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"types", skill:"apply", lowYield:true,
 concept:"cyp2c19-star3-stop-codon", tags:["smith"], source:"slide",
 stem:"CYP2C19*3 changes G to A so that the codon for tryptophan becomes a stop codon. What is the functional effect?",
 options:[
  {t:"No enzyme activity", correct:true, why:"Protein synthesis terminates; the slide lists proton pump inhibitors (omeprazole, lansoprazole) as affected drugs."},
  {t:"Normal enzyme activity", correct:false, why:"A premature stop truncates the protein."},
  {t:"Increased enzyme activity", correct:false, why:"Not the slide's effect."},
  {t:"A different amino acid only", correct:false, why:"That is a non-synonymous SNP; here the chain stops."},
  {t:"No change in the protein", correct:false, why:"That is a synonymous SNP."}],
 teach:"A premature stop codon SNP changes a codon so that it no longer codes for an amino acid but ends the chain. In CYP2C19*3 a G>A change turns the tryptophan codon into a stop; protein synthesis terminates and the enzyme has no activity. Affected drugs on the slide are proton pump inhibitors such as omeprazole and lansoprazole.",
 quote:"We might not get through the different types of polymorphisms today, but that's OK.",
 note:"Low yield: slide p. 36 was not reached on 10/20. Outside Friday's exam by the class vote.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slide ‘Premature Stop Codon SNP: CYP2C19’ (p. 36); not reached on 10/20 (transcript 10/20)"},

{id:"L17-060", lecture:"L17", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L17", sub:"types", skill:"tell", lowYield:true,
 concept:"cyp2d6-deletion-vs-copies", tags:["smith"], source:"slide",
 stem:"CYP2D6*5 and CYP2D6*2XN are both CYP2D6 variants. Which phenotype does each produce?",
 options:[
  {t:"*5: poor metabolizer; *2XN: ultrarapid metabolizer", correct:true, why:"*5 deletes the whole gene (null activity); *2XN carries extra copies of the gene."},
  {t:"*5: ultrarapid metabolizer; *2XN: poor metabolizer", correct:false, why:"Reversed."},
  {t:"Both: poor metabolizer", correct:false, why:"Extra copies increase activity."},
  {t:"Both: ultrarapid metabolizer", correct:false, why:"A deleted gene has no activity."},
  {t:"*5: normal; *2XN: poor metabolizer", correct:false, why:"Neither matches the slides."}],
 teach:"CYP2D6*5 is a gene deletion: thousands of base pairs making up the CYP2D6 gene are deleted, giving loss of function and a poor metabolizer (PM) phenotype. CYP2D6*2XN has extra copies of the gene (N = 2, 3, 4, 5 or 13), giving an ultrarapid metabolizer (UM) phenotype. The slides list SSRIs, tamoxifen, codeine and beta blockers as affected drugs for both.",
 quote:"We could have an entire gene deleted out of a person, OK? We do have that happen",
 note:"Low yield: slides pp. 39–40 were not reached on 10/20; she mentioned whole-gene deletion and extra copies only as kinds of polymorphism (L17-033). Outside Friday's exam by the class vote.",
 cite:"Intro_to_PGx_Lecture_Students_Fall_2026.pdf slides ‘Gene Deletion CYP2D6’ (p. 39), ‘Copy Number Variant CYP2D6’ (p. 40); not reached on 10/20 (transcript 10/20)"}
);
